{
 "question": "Where does the time of a decode of the AC-3 family go now, and what of the inverse transform can be had without moving a sample?",
 "method": "Timers around the stages of the decoder, in a throwaway build, over `verify --decode` of three streams, which makes the samples. Counters in another, over nine streams, for the blocks the transform is given that are zero or half zero. Then the inverse FFT inside the transform written four ways, all in one binary with a kernel for each, so that the figures are of the ways and not of four builds; each way was held to the others bit for bit on 1 536 blocks before it was timed. The way taken was measured as the kernel on main and with the change, as the binary of main against the binary of the change (`tools/bench.py --compare`, five runs), on two streams bound to the fast cores, and beside FFmpeg (`tools/bench.py --refs`). That no sample moved is a test in the repository, which holds the transform to the clause computed the plain way, and a run over the corpus (`tools/old_new.py --decode-all`), which compares every delivery byte for byte. The streams are media and are not in the repository.",
 "machine": {
  "processor": "Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700K",
  "logical_processors": 28,
  "system": "Windows 11"
 },
 "binaries": {
  "main before the change": "fa512e3266e76fb38d7d3504e0efd3c43223584ac7e73ecd41f6b9c2763d40b1",
